I started working on this feature a year ago.
In May last year, I completed the coding and testing of the first version and sent the patches privately to Hannes for review.
I used to work at SUSE for a few years and was a colleague of his.
I appreciate his positive feedback, professional review comments, and suggestions on how to move this forward.
Also thanks to Matthieu, Mat and Paolo from MPTCP community for reviewing the patches.
Currently, the implementation and testing phases are largely complete.
The code is currently in the RFC stage and has undergone several rounds of discussion and iteration on the MPTCP mailing list.

The modifications in the NVMe subsystem are minimal.
Most of the code change is on the MPTCP side.

The entire implementation consists of over 40 patches,
It mainly includes six sets:

1 mptcp: implement .read_sock

'NVMe over TCP' uses read_sock interface for data receiving.
However, it was not implemented in MPTCP, since the primary use cases for MPTCP are in userspace, where read_sock is not used.
So my first task was to implement this interface for MPTCP.

After 15 versions of iteration, this set has been merged into 7.0 mainline kernel in February this year.

2 NVME over MPTCP

This set adds basic MPTCP support for NVMe, but without ktls encryption.
It defines a new transport for MPTCP, which creates MPTCP connections between the target and the host instead of TCP connections.
This set is currently under review, in v8, and today's discussion is mainly about this set.

3 MPTCP KTLS support

MPTCP did not support KTLS. They are conflicting to some extent.
Because both MPTCP and KTLS use the ULP (Upper Layer Protocol) mechanism.
So ULP settings include TLS settings are disabled in MPTCP.
To support NVMe MPTCP, it's time to enable it.

I finally found a solution to make MPTCP and KTLS compatible, and implemented KTLS encryption support for MPTCP.
Need to say, only software KTLS encryption is supported for MPTCP right now; hardware KTLS encryption has not yet been developed.

This set has undergone 12 version iterations, and I believe it will be merged upstream this year.

4 Enable KTLS for NVME MPTCP

Once KTLS is supported for MPTCP, using TLS for NVMe MPTCP becomes straightforward.
it only requires enabling the TLS setting in configfs for MPTCP transport.

5 User space tools

User space libraries and tools, including libnvme, nvme-cli, and ktls-utils, need to add MPTCP support.

6 MPTCP corner cases fixes

During the development of NVMe MPTCP, some MPTCP corner cases bugs were triggered and need to be fixed.

That's all the patch list.

