'''

Let's take a look at a demo.

This demo is the selftest script for NVME MPTCP,
it is the last patch in the "NVME over MPTCP" patch set.

This script takes three arguments.
The first argument is the transport type, which can be set to "tcp" or "mptcp".
The second is the I/O policy, which can be set to "numa", "round-robin", or "queue-depth".
The third is "tls", which controls whether TLS encryption is used.

This test creates two network namespaces.
One is used to run the NVMe target, which is the server side,
and the other is used to run the NVMe host, which is the client side.

Each namespace has four virtual network devices,
and each network device is limited to a speed of 100 megabytes per second.

When we run NVMe TCP, the fio benchmark shows the speed is about 100 megabytes per second, as we expected.

That means only the first network device on both sides is used for NVMe TCP communication,
and the other three network devices are unaware of NVMe TCP.

When using MPTCP, this is not the case at all.

