'''

Before running NVMe MPTCP, we need to configure the IP addresses to tell MPTCP which addresses are available.

We can use the "ip" command to perform such configuration.
On both sides, we use "ip mptcp endpoint add" to add the IP addresses of the second,
third, and fourth network devices to the MPTCP path manager address list

The difference is, on the target side, the commands use "signal" flag.
While on the host side, use "subflow" flag.

After the configuration, we can run NVMe MPTCP now.
The fio benchmark shows the speed reached about 400 megabytes per second.
That means all four network devices on both sides are used for the NVMe MPTCP communication.

How is all this achieved? Let's look at the next slide.

