Introduction to MPTCP

- MPTCP is an extension to the standard TCP and is defined in RFC 8684.
- TCP Option, MPTCP Kind Number is 30.
- It lets a device use multiple network interfaces at the same time to send and receive TCP packets over a single connection.
- combine the bandwidth, choose the best one, fail-over
- introduction in Linux kernel v5.6
- ip mptcp endpoint add 10.1.2.2 subflow
- int fd = socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_MPTCP);

For more details, please visit the project website: https://www.mptcp.dev.

'''

Here I will give a very brief introduction to MPTCP.

MPTCP or Multipath TCP, is an extension to the standard TCP and is defined in RFC 8684.
It lets a device use multiple network interfaces at the same time to send and receive TCP packets over a single connection.
MPTCP can combine the bandwidth of multiple interfaces or choose the best one with the lowest latency.
It also supports fail-over, if one path goes down, traffic is smoothly switched to another path without interruption.

Since its introduction in Linux kernel v5.6

For more details, please visit the project website: https://www.mptcp.dev.

