'''
I took a very engineering-style approach to implement this feature:
make changes, test, fix test failures, test again, fix more test failures
and only after all issues are resolved, go back and think about the code architecture.

Following the same approach I used when adding MPTCP support to applications,
I first modified the socket creation code for both the NVMe target and host sides,
changing IPPROTO_TCP to IPPROTO_MPTCP to create MPTCP sockets,
and then implemented a test script to run it.

Then I got a list_add corruption crash.
During debugging, I found that it is inside tcp_sock_set_nodelay,
I need to implement an MPTCP version of set_nodelay function.

After fixing the issue, I continued testing and found that sometimes bind() would report an 'Address already in use' error,
so I then implemented an MPTCP version of the set_reuseaddr function.

Next, I found that sometimes nvme disconnect in the test would fail.
During debugging, I found that sock_no_linger was not set successfully,
so I implemented an MPTCP version of the no_linger function.

In the end, I realized that all six functions that set socket behavior needed to have MPTCP versions implemented,
and then the tests became stable.

I abstracted all the places that needed to be changed from TCP to MPTCP into a structure,
and then defined different instances for TCP and MPTCP to switch between them.

However, because there are slight differences between the functions on the target side and the host side,
it ended up being separated into two structures: one for the target and one for the host.
Next, let's look at the target one first.

'''
